ived_by":null}],"state":"closed","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":2,"created_at":"2025-01-27T21:52:50Z","updated_at":"2025-01-28T12:28:07Z","closed_at":"2025-01-28T12:28:06Z","assignee":null,"author_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\n- Reproducible in v4.1stable, v4.2stable, v4.3.stable, v4.4.dev1 and later v4.4 snapshots\n\n### System information\n\nGodot v4.3.stable - Windows 10.0.22631 - Vulkan (Forward+) - dedicated NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060 (NVIDIA; 31.0.15.5132) - 12th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-12700F (20 Threads)\n\n### Issue description\n\nWhen using the AnimationPlayer to animate a Camera3D's FOV, using cubic interpolation causes overshooting of keyframes. The FOV should be held constant between two consecutive keyframes of the same value, but using cubic interpolation causes continual zooming beyond the desired point. This effect is exaggerated the longer the period between the keyframes of the same value.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nTo reproduce:\n1. Create Camera3D and AnimationPlayer nodes.\n2. Add keyframes for the Camera3D's FOV, one with value A, then two consecutive keyframes with value B.\n3. Play the animation in the scene player.\n\nTo use MRP:\n1. Open MRP.\n2. Play scene.\n3. Observe the camera continuing to zoom beyond the keyframe at the one second mark.\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\n[camera-fov-tweening-bug.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/18564918/camera-fov-tweening-bug.zip)","closed_by":{"login":"TokageItLab","id":61938263,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjYxOTM4MjYz","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/61938263?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/TokageItLab","html_url":"https://github.com/TokageItLab","followers_url":